#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# to_polar_coordinates

#% text
# `to_polar_coordinates([x, y])` gives the polar coordinates [r, theta] of the point; `to_polar_coordinates([x1, ..., xn])` the hyperspherical coordinates [r, theta1, ..., theta_(n-1)], the inverse of `from_polar_coordinates`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# r = |x|; theta_k = acos(x_k/sqrt(x_k^2 + ... + x_n^2)) for k < n - 1, and the last angle is `atan(x_(n-1), x_n)`, in (-pi, pi]. A list of points maps.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
to_polar_coordinates([1, 2])
#% output
#= [5^(1/2), atan(2)]

#% cell
to_polar_coordinates([1, 1, 1])
#% output
#= [3^(1/2), acos((1/3)*3^(1/2)), (1/4)*pi]

#% cell
to_polar_coordinates([[1, 0], [0, -2], [-1, -1]])
#% output
#= [[1, 0], [2, (-1/2)*pi], [2^(1/2), (-3/4)*pi]]

#% cell
n(from_polar_coordinates(to_polar_coordinates([3, -4, 12])))
#% output
#= [3.0, -3.999999999999999, 12.000000000000002]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `from_polar_coordinates`, `abs_arg`
